  "conclusion": {
    "observed": [
      "All 4,096 train and 512 held-out latent records preserve exact categorical actions, rewards, terminals, record indices and source raw-tree identities.",
      "The dynamics run started from scratch from clean detached source ed5ee9d82721ab9e868f900123a44b83315ffdea.",
      "Before the infrastructure interruption, the run emitted finite metrics through 62,601 completed updates, fixed validation videos through 60,001 updates and a durable step-50k checkpoint; the explicitly authorized recovery preserved this history and replayed updates 50,001 through 62,601 under a new attempt ID.",
      "The recovered run reached exactly 200,000 completed updates and checkpoint step 199,999; the final checkpoint contains 62 files, 99,518,770 bytes and has project-canonical tree SHA256 7fa36878716d9d943a947882402a9e27437707f1fbd64022e264a67e3d72485c.",
      "Median recorder throughput over non-null progress samples from updates 1,001 through 10,000 was 6.5986 updates/s from offline latents versus 4.0157 updates/s from CR-DYN-0010 raw RGB, a 1.6432x speedup above the preregistered 1.25x gate.",
      "On 128 original-RGB shortcut videos, mean-frame PSNR was 24.065977 dB and mean SSIM was 0.833979; on eight 256-step full-diffusion videos they were 24.053035 dB and 0.846804.",
      "In the fixed 64-video action control, aligned actions reached 26.210389 dB at horizon 16 and exceeded batch-shuffled and all-noop actions by 7.806056 and 6.480551 dB. All preregistered numeric quality and action-use thresholds passed."
    ],
    "interpretation": [
      "Offline latent encoding preserved the audited transition protocol, removed repeated tokenizer work and materially accelerated the same dynamics training recipe. The terminal metric and action-control gates support the preregistered infrastructure hypothesis, while the replayed 50,001–62,601 interval remains a recorded fault-recovery deviation rather than unique extra budget."
    ],
